{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:60e349e98f5f43d53d65d38b13bd0b457ca2715a12d7829181259c6e6aee737e","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.13,"B":0.53,"C":0.22,"D":0.12}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR: Historian's 17 / 53 / 13 / 17. Applied the lexical-priority lock, first match wins, against MY OWN CQ3 (0.85 / 1.80 / 2.75, fitted approximately normal with sigma = (2.75-1.80)/1.2816 = 0.741) and my own OECD-38 GDP-weighted core path.\n\nSTEP 1, A (coupled growth >= 2.6%/yr): P = 1 - Phi((2.6-1.80)/0.741) = 1 - Phi(1.080) = 0.140, trimmed to 13 for the left skew (demographic denominators are near-deterministic over ten years, so the right tail is thinner than Gaussian). A is PINNED to my own CQ3 right tail, not chosen. Cross-check: 13 sits just below the standalone AI-Q6 Transformative Breakout at 16.2%, coherent because coupled A can be reached by China upside without a TFP breakout but is made harder by the locked aging drag now being endogenous. UPDATE: 17 → 13, moderated, purely mechanical on my lower P50.\n\nSTEP 2, C (OECD-38 GDP-weighted core 2032-36 >= 2.7% AND growth < 2.0%): THIS IS WHERE MY SEAT DIVERGES MOST FROM ITS PRIOR, and the reason is the CQ2 conjunct-A structural correction, not evidence. My 2027-2036 core central is ~2.50-2.55% with a front-loaded path (2027-2030 ~2.9% on grid/power capex crowding, the tariff/fragmentation LEVEL shift amortized, electricity pass-through into business input costs, the first care-wage wave; 2031-2036 ~2.35-2.40% on automated professional/information/financial services at ~1.0%, post-bust excess capacity, China overcapacity export deflation, the BIS Box C labour-income demand drag). CRITICALLY, the ~0.55-0.62pp contributed by the five